{"causes":["Tasso began writing Jerusalem Delivered in the 1570s, influenced by the cultural and political climate of Renaissance Italy.","The First Crusade, which began in 1095, had a profound impact on the Middle East and Europe, shaping the course of medieval history.","The publication of Jerusalem Delivered in 1581 helped establish Tasso as a major literary figure in Italy and beyond.","The rise of the Ottoman Empire in the 14th century created a complex geopolitical landscape that influenced the development of Jerusalem Delivered.","The Catholic Church's influence on the medieval world, particularly in Italy, contributed to the poem's themes and imagery.","The cultural and artistic movements of the Renaissance, such as Humanism and Mannerism, influenced Tasso's style and themes in Jerusalem Delivered.","The literary and intellectual circles of Florence and Rome, where Tasso lived and worked, played a significant role in the poem's creation and dissemination.","The economic and social changes of the Renaissance, including the growth of cities and trade, impacted the poem's themes and characters.","The historical events of the 15th century, including the Italian Wars and the rise of the Habsburg Empire, influenced the poem's context and reception.","The cultural and linguistic diversity of Italy in the 15th century, including the presence of Arabic, Greek, and Latin, shaped the poem's themes and imagery."],"effects":["Jerusalem Delivered helped establish the epic poem as a major genre in Italian literature.","The poem's portrayal of the First Crusade and the capture of Jerusalem has had a lasting impact on Western historical narratives.","Tasso's use of classical models and themes in Jerusalem Delivered influenced the development of Western literature, particularly in the 16th and 17th centuries.","The poem's exploration of themes such as chivalry, honor, and the struggle between good and evil has continued to resonate with readers and scholars.","The publication of Jerusalem Delivered in 1581 helped establish Tasso as a major literary figure in Italy and beyond.","The poem's influence can be seen in the works of later poets, writers, and artists, such as Shakespeare and Milton.","The poem's themes and imagery have been interpreted and reinterpreted in various cultural and historical contexts.","Jerusalem Delivered has had a lasting impact on Western literature, particularly in the genres of poetry, drama, and opera.","The poem's exploration of the complex relationships between politics, religion, and culture has continued to influence literary and historical analysis.","The poem's influence can be seen in the development of Western art and architecture, particularly in the 16th and 17th centuries."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Torquato Tasso","sourceName":"Torquato Tasso","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"Tasso began writing Jerusalem Delivered in the 1570s, influenced by the cultural and political climate of Renaissance Italy."},{"sourceSlug":"Godfrey of Bouillon","sourceName":"Godfrey of Bouillon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"The poem portrays Godfrey of Bouillon as the leader of the Christian knights who capture Jerusalem."},{"sourceSlug":"First Crusade","sourceName":"First Crusade","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"The poem tells a mythified account of the First Crusade and the capture of Jerusalem."},{"sourceSlug":"Catholic Church","sourceName":"Catholic Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"The Catholic Church's influence on the medieval world, particularly in Italy, contributed to the poem's themes and imagery."},{"sourceSlug":"Renaissance Italy","sourceName":"Renaissance Italy","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"The cultural and political climate of Renaissance Italy influenced Tasso's writing of Jerusalem Delivered."},{"sourceSlug":"Humanism","sourceName":"Humanism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"The literary and intellectual movements of Humanism influenced Tasso's style and themes in Jerusalem Delivered."},{"sourceSlug":"Mannerism","sourceName":"Mannerism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Jerusalem Delivered","targetName":"Jerusalem Delivered","context":"The cultural and artistic movements of Mannerism shaped Tasso's themes and imagery in Jerusalem Delivered."}],"places":[{"name":"Florence","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Rome","role":"Residence"},{"name":"Jerusalem","role":"Subject of conquest"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Jerusalem Delivered","type":"Reference article","slug":"jerusalem-delivered"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1053313","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerusalem_Delivered"],"tags":["literary work","Epic Poetry & Mythology","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Jerusalem Delivered contributed to the historical record of the Early Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Jerusalem Delivered (Italian: Gerusalemme Liberata) is an Italian epic poem by Torquato Tasso (1544–1595), published in 1581 (an authorised edition of the text Tasso had been circulating in manuscript for years). The poem narrates the First Crusade (1095–1099) and the capture of Jerusalem in twenty cantos of ottava rima (eight-line stanzas in iambic elevens), centred on the Christian commander Goffredo di Buglione (Godfrey of Bouillon) and his efforts to unite the Crusader armies for the final assault on Jerusalem, complicated by the love affairs, distractions, and interventions of both Christian and Muslim warriors, pagan sorceresses, and the supernatural agency of God and Satan.

Jerusalem Delivered is the masterwork of the Italian Renaissance epic tradition and the final major product of the Counter-Reformation's attempt to unite Christian heroic values with classical epic form. Tasso consciously modelled his poem on both the Iliad and the Aeneid while subordinating classical epic conventions to the demands of Christian morality and Counter-Reformation orthodoxy — a project that generated the poem's central tension between the classical demands of epic (erotic episodes, heroic individualism, pagan magic) and the Christian demands of religious seriousness.

Jerusalem Delivered was enormously influential across European literature — it was the primary source for Peter Paul Rubens and Nicolas Poussin (both painted its scenes), it generated decades of operatic adaptations (Handel's Rinaldo, 1711, was based on Tasso's characters), and it was deeply read by Edmund Spenser (whose Faerie Queene is indebted to Tasso), John Milton (Paradise Lost echoes Tasso's Christian epic), and Voltaire (whose Henriade was modelled on Tasso). The poem's fame rested partly on Tasso's tragic biography — he spent seven years in an asylum at Sant'Anna in Ferrara (1579–1586), generating the Romantic myth of the 'mad genius' poet.","subjects":["Italian Literature","Renaissance Era","Torquato Tasso","Epic Poetry","Counter-Reformation","Opera","Crusades Literature","European Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Jerusalem Delivered is a significant work of Italian literature that explores the themes of chivalry, honor, and the struggle between good and evil in the context of the First Crusade.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
